  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Application Structure**\n\nOkay, so I got the type-checking to pass without a hitch! Now, I\'m diving into the application\'s skeletal framework. I\'m starting by enumerating the core types, such as the `NavTab`, which I\'ve defined to include options like \'soc\', \'scan\', \'logs\', \'sentinel\', and \'config\'. This is the basis to figure out the needed components.\n\n\n**Enumerating Core Data Types**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously defining the crucial data structures for the application. Key types such as `ThreatVerdict`, `Finding`, and `UrlFeature` are now established. I\'ve also fleshed out the `TelemetryRecord`, `PlaybookModule`, `ChatMessage`, and `ConfigSettings` to ensure robust data handling throughout the system.\n\n\n**Defining Configuration Objects**\n\nI\'ve been working on fleshing out the application\'s configuration settings and core components. I\'ve got the configuration objects defined, covering aspects like typosquatting detection and webhooks. Now, I\'m onto modular design: building the `Header.tsx` component, including the hamburger menu and the PhishGuard logo and the title and subtitle logic.\n\n\n**Designing UI Components**\n\nI\'ve made significant progress on the UI elements. The hamburger menu is planned to reveal a drawer with node info, active cluster status, quick actions, and fast navigation. The bottom navigation bar, with SOC, Scan, Logs, Sentinel, and Config icons, is styled as per the design with active indicators. Moreover, the first screen of the SOC command center is outlined, including the status banner, KPI cards, and quick URL ingestion drawer.\n\n\n**Visualizing Data Display**\n\nI\'m now focused on visualizing the data output. I\'ve got an SVG donut gauge planned, displaying threat categorization percentages. Below that, a live intercept feed with clickable cards is envisioned, allowing users to dive into the URL scanner. I\'m also planning a SOC Defense Manual carousel and fleshing out the `NeuralUrlScanner` screen, including an omnibar, verdict banner, and neural threat model.\n\n\n**Detailing Risk Assessment**\n\nNow, I\'m fleshing out the risk assessment dial and associated metrics. I\'m focusing on the SVG gauge for threat categorization. I\'ve broken down the sub-metrics, core findings, and the Deep URL Feature Matrix. Tactical remediation buttons, such as \"Quarantine & Block\" and \"Export STIX IOC,\" are also included, which trigger real actions. Also, there\'s a section on TelemetryHistory.tsx.\n\n\n**Refining User Filtering**\n\nI\'m now honing the filtering capabilities within the Telemetry Records list. I\'ve designed an omnibar for instant filtering by domain, IP, or hash. I\'m also including filter pills for verdict types (all verdicts, malicious, etc.), time range (last 24h), and a way to export to CSV. There will also be a telemetry records list with checkboxes for bulk actions, a sticky tactical bar, and a forensics modal overlay for deep dives. This includes details like registrar information and DNS TTL. The AI Assistant Screen 4 is being outlined as well.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 638715966
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
