    thinking: "**Analyzing the Prompt**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously dissecting the user\'s prompt. It seems the core request is a UI redesign of the PhishGuard AI dashboard, specifically the removal of the bottom navigation bar due to duplication. I understand the importance of retaining functionality while overhauling the user interface. My focus is on the critical fix: the navigation bar.\n\n\n**Revising the Navigation**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to the sidebar navigation. It\'ll be a fixed, vertical element on the left, with the suggested widths and border. The dark charcoal background is locked in, and the color scheme will be followed, especially regarding the AI-related purple/violet accents. I\'m focusing on \"Dashboard,\" \"URL Scanner,\" \"Scan History,\" \"AI Security Chatbot,\" \"Security Tips,\" and \"Settings\" within. I\'m working on the clean brand.\n\n\n**Defining the Visual Identity**\n\nI\'m solidifying the sidebar\'s top section. The PhishGuard AI logo and title are in place, accompanied by the subtitle \"SOC THREAT INTELLIGENCE.\" I\'ve added a status indicator pill to display either \"AUTONOMOUS CORE\" or \"LIVE MONITORING,\" complete with a pulsating teal/cyan dot for the latter. Now I\'m carefully organizing the six requested navigation items, ensuring each has a distinct icon and clear label.\n\n\n**Implementing the UI**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the practical implementation. The focus is on ensuring a seamless user experience. I\'ve completely removed any trace of the bottom navigation bar and have fully deleted the file and removed any references to it. The top header is coming together, too, with its breadcrumbs, search bar, and key buttons. The redesigned dashboard is also getting my full attention.\n\n\n**Integrating New Dashboard Elements**\n\nI\'m now integrating the requested elements into the redesigned dashboard layout. The top header is being carefully integrated to complement the main page. I\'m focusing on the \"Scan URL\" button\'s prominent placement in both the header and the dashboard\'s quick scan bar. I\'m currently designing the metric cards: Total Scans, Safe Websites, Suspicious Websites, and Phishing Websites, ensuring the correct color accents. Finally, I\'m beginning the implementation of the \"Risk Analytics Chart,\" a sleek visualization showcasing scan activity and threat interception.\n\n\n**Designing the Analytics**\n\nI\'m now deep in designing the Risk Analytics Chart\'s visual elements, with a focus on clear data representation. I\'m building a threat distribution donut chart, outlining Safe, Suspicious, and Phishing website percentages with a clean legend. I\'m also adding attack vector frequency bars for key threats such as Typosquatting, Credential Harvesters, and Zero-Day SSL Bypass. Next, I am creating a \"Recent Scan Activity\" section with an interactive table, showing real-time scan data.\n\n\n\n**Developing the Security Tips**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on the \"Security Tips\" section. I\'m prioritizing interactive cards that link to a detailed playbook when users click \"Read Playbook\". I\'m populating the section with key tips: identifying Punycode attacks, detecting AiTM proxy links, analyzing zero-day certificate age, and MFA fatigue defenses. The goal is easy access to key security defense knowledge. I\'m focusing on the dedicated \"Security Tips\" view, too.\n\n\n"
